<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">El Tajín</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1200 CE (approx.) · capital El Tajín</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading city of north-central Veracruz, renowned for the Pyramid of the Niches and for its many ballcourts carved with scenes of sacrifice.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>El Tajín grew in the hills of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, in a region of rich rainfall and tropical farming. It became a significant centre from about 600 CE and reached its greatest size after the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan</a>, dominating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> region later known as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/totonac-state-of-cempoala">Totonacapan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-niches">Pyramid of the Niches</a>, with 365 recesses, and the Tajín Chico palace area display a distinctive style of stepped frets and flying cornices. At least 17 ballcourts have been found; panels in the South Ballcourt show players, sacrifice and the gods. A ruler named 13 Rabbit appears on columns of the Building of the Columns. The economy probably relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and vanilla.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>El Tajín declined and was largely abandoned around 1200, possibly after burning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totonacs">Totonac</a> people later dominated the region. It was rediscovered in 1785 and declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1992.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/el-tajin.jpg" alt="El Tajín" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Taj%C3%ADn_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>El Tajín</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>13 Rabbit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably Totonac</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mexico</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>El Tajin, Tajín</dd></dl></aside></div>

